## [:< ##

# name  = users.record.optional_fields
# descr = fields a record MAY carry but is not created with
# param = -none-

## the authority's vocabulary of OPTIONAL fields, alongside
## users.record.default_fields' mandatory ones.  a record is created with the
## defaults ; these are what a user-edit console offers to ADD, and what any
## other client sees as the same vocabulary rather than inventing its own
## ad-hoc key names.
##
## NAMES MUST MATCH ^\w+$ -- they become ascii.frame slot tokens, and
## user-edit.form.schema_from_record skips anything else, so 'full_user_name'
## rather than 'full-user-name'.  the value is the field's STARTING SHAPE, and
## the shape IS the declaration : [] makes a collapsible multi-value list
## exactly as contact is, '' makes a plain line.
##
## EXTEND HERE.  this list is deliberately small -- it is a starting
## vocabulary, not a closed model, and adding to it costs one line.

## split from a single 'full_name', per user 2026-08-12 : the USER-facing name
## [ what a git commit or a login carries -- 'Taeki Ten' ] and the REAL name [
## what a job application reply pins to a specific email/domain instead ] are
## two different pieces of data with two different audiences, not one field
## with two use cases
return {
    qw| full_user_name | => '',
    qw| full_real_name | => '',
    qw| note |           => '',
    qw| phone |          => [],
    qw| shell |          => '',
    qw| timezone |       => '',
};

